**Current support.** `config.moth` uses ordinary named `project` and selected builder-settings records with `$config` input declarations. The named records remain the accepted configuration model. `$config` marks typed inputs and does not replace the records. See @../progress (Progress).

`config.moth` is optional at the selected build root and is one self-contained compile-time source with no source dependencies. A nested config file has no effect on its enclosing build.

### Project and builder settings

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    project #= (
        name = "my_site",
        version = "1.0.0",
    )

    html #= ()
]

The `project` record is optional. Omitting `name` uses the selected directory name and omitting `version` means unversioned. The HTML builder section is also optional and uses defaults when omitted. The command selects the builder; these records never select one. There is no `entry_root` setting.

### Typed configuration inputs

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    $config
    version #String = "1.0.0"

    project #= (
        version = version,
    )

    html #= ()
]

`$config` marks one explicitly typed top-level compile-time `#` binding as a build input. The binding name is the input key, the declared type is the receiving contract and the initializer is the missing-input fallback. Only a marked binding may omit its initializer. The rest of `config.moth` remains ordinary compile-time configuration. `$project`, `$builder` and `$html_builder` are not accepted directives.

See @#project-config (Project config), @#build-inputs (Build configuration values) and @../directives (Directives).
